	affiliations = {Biosystem and Bioprocess Engineering Laboratory, Department of Microbiology, M.D. University, Haryana, Rohtak, 124001, India},
	abstract = {Cancer remains a major global health challenge, with breast cancer being the most prevalent malignancy in women and a leading cause of cancer-related mortality. This study employed an integrative in silico approach to analyse anticancer cyclotides, aiming to identify key physicochemical features associated with their potency and to explore their interactions with breast cancer targets. The study compared cyclotides for their physicochemical properties, sequence variations, and structural features, and identified key determinants of anticancer activity. Statistical analysis of eleven properties of potent and non-potent cyclotides was conducted, including molecular weight, isoelectric point (pI), net charge, GRAVY score, and counts of basic, acidic, aliphatic, aromatic, polar, and non-polar residues, as well as total residue count. The results showed that potent cyclotides exhibited significantly higher values of net charge, pI, GRAVY score, molecular weight, and higher counts of basic, aliphatic, non-polar, and total residues. Principal Component Analysis (PCA) was also applied to these features. Post-PCA analysis revealed that the principal components (PC1 and PC2) correlated with IC₅₀ values, clustering potent cyclotides and thereby highlighting the significance of these properties. Furthermore, molecular docking and dynamics simulations were conducted to investigate the interactions of selected cyclotides with two key breast cancer receptors, Estrogen Receptor (ER-α) and Epidermal Growth Factor Receptor (EGFR), to identify the amino acid interactions that drive receptor activation and conformational changes. Cliotide T12 (cT12) and Vibi G emerged as strong binders, with cT12 exhibiting superior binding stability and interaction profiles. In contrast, Vibi G showed weaker dynamics, especially with ER-α. These results reveal cT12 as a promising lead for further preclinical development in breast cancer therapy. © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to