	abstract = {Clitoria ternatea L. (Fabaceae), the butterfly pea, is a perennial leguminous forage and cover-crop legume that has recently attracted growing interest as an industrial crop, beyond its long-documented ethnomedicinal and nutraceutical reputation. This review reframes the evidence base around the industrial dimensions of C. ternatea: its agronomic performance as a nitrogen-fixing forage and soil-restoration legume; its genetic diversity as a resource for cultivar development; and the extraction technologies underlying commercial production of its two most industrially significant metabolites, the polyacylated anthocyanins (ternatins) and the cyclic cystine-knot peptides (cyclotides). It further examines the development and regulatory registration of the cyclotide-based biopesticide Sero-X®, and emerging bio-based applications, including anthocyanin-responsive intelligent packaging and antibacterial bacterial-cellulose composite films. Non-food value chains in natural dyes, cosmetics, and animal feed are also considered. Functional and pharmacological properties (antioxidant, antimicrobial, antidiabetic) are summarised briefly as mechanistic rationale for these industrial applications rather than as the review's primary focus. A systematic search of Scopus, Web of Science, and PubMed (1983–June 2026) yielded 68 primary sources meeting defined inclusion criteria. Critical evidence gaps include the scarcity of multi-location, multi-season agronomic field trials; the absence of standardised, industrial-scale extraction protocols; limited pilot- and field-scale validation of bio-based packaging; and underdeveloped regulatory pathways for ternatins as colorants and cyclotide-based biopesticides outside Australia. Priorities for future work include omics-guided cultivar development for enhanced ternatin and cyclotide yield, techno-economic assessment of extraction and formulation processes, and expanded field-scale trials of biopesticide and packaging applicati