	affiliations = {Department of Bioinformatics, Christ College, Gujarat, Rajkot, India; Coventry University, Coventry, United Kingdom; School of Science, RK University, Gujarat, Rajkot, India; Department of Bioinformatics, University of North Bengal, West Bengal, Darjeeling, India},
	abstract = {Plant peptide toxins are bioactive compounds produced by plants with pharmacological or toxicological effects on humans/animals. Cyclotides exhibit a range of therapeutic activities, including uterotonic, hemolytic, antineurotensin, anti-HIV, cytotoxic, antifouling, and anticancer. In addition, cyclotides have also been reported for biopesticide activities. Their solid three-dimensional fold makes them enormously stable toward thermal, chemical, and enzymatic degradation. Cyclotides are mainly found in the coffee (Rubiaceae) and violet (Violaceae) plant families. In Violaceae, cyclotides are extensively distributed among the genus Leonia and Viola, but the cyclotides complement the vast majority of Violaceae species that have not yet been explored. Though they exhibit a variety of bioactivities by nature, mutation studies were also carried out to determine the influence of individual residues in the protein. This chapter reviews the past and current understanding of cyclotides’ biology and drug discovery of the genera Leonia and Viola, under the Violaceae family. © 2024 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.},
