	abstract = {Antimicrobial peptides, also known as AMPs, are short chains of 20-40 amino acids characterized by their high basicity and amphilicity. These peptides are naturally occurring in various plant parts such as stems, roots, and leaves, where they serve as an innate defense mechanism against viruses, parasites, fungi, and bacteria. They are ribosomal gene-encoded prepeptides and include families including cyclotides, lipid-transfer proteins, snakins, thionins, hairpinins, knottins, and hevein-like peptides. Due to their wide efficacy by adhering to lipid membranes and forming ion channels, particularly against bacterial invaders, research on AMPs regarding their extraction, purification, and appropriate isolation is being done to characterise them as contributors of therapeutic and preservative agents. AMP purification, first done in 1970 standardised three stages, plant material homogenization, extraction, and purification. At present, the purification process requires optimization for individual AMPs to achieve the best results. This involves various methods such as absorption-desorption, salt precipitation, reversed-phase high-performance liquid chromatography, a combination of ion-exchange and reversed-phase C18 solid phase extraction, sodium dodecyl sulfate polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis, and even chitin affinity chromatography for chitin-binding AMPs. © 2026 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.},
