	abstract = {Polypeptides having four or more cysteine residues in their sequences may be regarded as cysteine-rich peptides (CRPs), whereby the minimum cysteine percentage can be approximately >10% for an average length >20 amino acid residues. There has been a steady increase in the number of reports on CRPs from plants during the last decade or so. This chapter focuses on CRPs identified from various plants belonging to families such as Fabaceae , Violaceae , Poaceae , and Brassicaceae . CRPs have been reported from leaves, seeds, roots, etc. The average intact molecular mass of plant CRPs is in the range ~ 2.5 kDa—~ 8 kDa. These are ribosomally synthesized peptides and are classified based on the cysteine pattern and disulfide connectivity. Several CRPs have been recognized as antimicrobial peptides (AMPs), and in the wake of rapidly emerging antibiotic or antimicrobial resistance (AMR), these peptides could become useful alternatives. A few CRPs are also cytotoxic, and some exhibit immunomodulatory activities. Additionally, they are touted to have important applications in agriculture, particularly as insecticides or fungicides. In this chapter, structural characteristics encompassing primary and three-dimensional molecular structures of four categories of CRPs, for example, cyclotides, defensins, thionins, and hevein-like peptides are highlighted. Molecular structural details of other CRPs from a few recent reports, for example, coffeetides, potentides, and ginsentides are also summarized. © 2027 selection and editorial matter Nisha Gaur, Santanu Sasidharan and Nagendra Singh; individual chapters, the contributors.},
	keywords = {Antibiotics; Indium compounds; Insecticides; Microorganisms; Plant diseases; Plants (botany); Seed; Sulfur compounds; Amino acid residues; Antimicrobial peptide; Average length; Brassicaceae; Classifieds; Cysteine residues; Disulfide connectivities; Fabaceae; Poaceae; Synthesised; Peptides},
