	abstract = {Human health and the world’s food supply security are now seriously threatened by the recent rise of antibiotic resistance against bacterial diseases. Plant antimicrobial peptides (AMPs) have lately drawn a substantial amount of curiosity as potential antibiotic substitutes. Plant AMPs are a subclass of short, typically polypeptides with a positive charge that are found in both animal and plant life as well as in humans; they are regarded as a segment of the plant’s barrier defense system and contribute to its defenses against a diversity of diseases, comprising nematodes, fungi, viruses, and bacteria. AMPs are intriguing applicants for human and animal disease treatment brought on by pathogens because of their distinctive antimicrobial features, including a wide range of effectiveness, quick death, specificity in targeting, great structural stability, and various mechanisms of action. They can be categorized into many families according to their molecular weight, effects in opposition to various microorganisms, structure, charge of the molecules, disulfide bond, and mode of action. Plant AMPs are divided into six prominent families by considering the number of cysteine residues and disulfide linkages. Plant AMPs have a diverse range of antimicrobial characteristics, which can permeate membranes and interfere with the creation of DNA, RNA, and proteins. These properties may make plant AMPs an effective strategy for preventing the appearance of bacterial resistance. The majority of naturally occurring antimicrobial peptides typically have 10-50 amino acids, are 2-9kDa in size, comprise amino acids that are extremely hydrophobic, and frequently have helical shapes. The amino acid sequence of cyclotides, lipid transfer proteins, defensins, thionins, snakins, and hevein-like proteins is identical to the set of antimicrobial peptides produced by plants. This chapter summarizes the key plant AMP families, including information on structure, purpose, mode of action, and antibacterial properties. © 2026 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.},
