	abstract = {Plant antimicrobial peptides are structurally diverse defense molecules that play essential roles in plant innate immunity and protection against microbial invasion. This review summarizes current advances in their biosynthesis, structural diversity, membrane-targeting mechanisms, and functions in plant–microbe interactions. Major peptide families, including defensins, thionins, lipid transfer proteins, cyclotides, hevein-like peptides, and related groups, are discussed alongside the emerging class of non-canonical antimicrobial peptides encoded by previously overlooked small open reading frames. Particular emphasis is placed on how precursor processing, structural organization, and peptide–membrane interactions govern antimicrobial activity and contribute to coordinated defense responses. The review also examines the integration of these peptides with broader plant defense networks and highlights recent progress in peptide engineering and translational applications for sustainable crop protection. Collectively, plant antimicrobial peptides represent promising biological tools for future agricultural and biotechnological innovation. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd.},
