	abstract = {Blocking the PD-1/PD-L1 interaction is an effective immunotherapeutic approach, yet monoclonal antibodies (mAbs) used for this purpose remain limited by high cost, restricted tissue penetration, and immune-related adverse effects. Cyclotides, a family of cyclic peptides derived from Viola odorata, represent promising alternatives owing to their structural rigidity, stability, and potential to disrupt protein-protein interactions. In this study, a library of 66 cyclotides from V. odorata was screened in silico against the PD-1 receptor. Molecular docking identified Phyb C and Cycloviolacin O7 as the top candidates, both binding within the PD-1 epitopes, with predicted binding energies of − 11.8 kcal/mol and − 11.6 kcal/mol, respectively. Molecular dynamics simulations of 1500 ns revealed that the PD-1_cyclotide complexes persisted over the simulation time. Furthermore, RMSD, Rg, RMSF, PCA, and free energy surface analyses, showed that cyclotide binding modulates PD-1 conformational dynamics as compared to PD-1 apo form. Binding free energies were further calculated using MM-PBSA and MM-GBSA approaches, incorporating entropic contributions estimated by normal mode analysis. Phyb C displayed favourable MM-PBSA and MM-GBSA binding free energies, with ΔG<sub>binding_PB</sub> of − 41.35 kcal/mol and ΔG<sub>binding_GB</sub> of − 39.14 kcal/mol, whereas Cycloviolacin O7 showed less favourable values, with ΔG<sub>binding_PB</sub> of − 25.65 kcal/mol and ΔG<sub>binding_GB</sub> of − 29.68 kcal/mol. Overall, these results suggest Phyb C as a promising cyclotide scaffold for PD-1 targeting. However, this study is entirely computational, and the functional inhibition of the PD-1/PD-L1 signaling pathway remains to be validated experimentally. © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.},
