	abstract = {Cyclotides are stable peptides that are amenable for grafting desirable therapeutic sequences into their disulfide-rich framework. A large number of grafted cyclotides that modulate receptor-mediated processes or inhibit protein–protein interactions have now been developed. Key protein targets are present inside cells and the ability to access this challenging therapeutic space is highly desirable for developing new drugs. Internalization studies have demonstrated that kalata B1, MCoTI-I, MCoTI-II, and acyclic analog EETI-II predominantly enter cells via endocytic pathways, with a proportion of the peptide gaining access to the cytosol. Being able to determine the intracellular location and cytosolic availability of cyclotide analogs is important for evaluating their suitability as therapeutic leads. In this chapter, we review the methodologies that have been used to describe cyclotide internalization and determine intracellular location, and provide a step-by-step approach to labeling peptides with a fluorescent probe, detecting internalized peptides using flow cytometry and determining intracellular localization using confocal microscopy. © 2021, Springer Science+Business Media, LLC, part of Springer Nature.},
